{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-80193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4=4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-2\\pi +x=-2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "287347f118da8b2a84761a8f163ea985659aa04f8751834606c661b7961e7d35"} {"question" "q-80193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2=2-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 0 & =-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},{90}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot (-90)=-8\\,100.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "287347f118da8b2a84761a8f163ea985659aa04f8751834606c661b7961e7d35"} {"question" "q-80197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16\\,200"] ["math" "-24\\,300"] ["math" "-8\\,100"] ["math" "16\\,200"] ["math" "32\\,400"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-80197" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8\\,100"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-120626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-120626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(-\\frac{3}{2})=-(56.309\\,9…)^{∘}≈-{56.310}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{56.310}^{∘}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{56.310}^{∘}={123.690}^{∘},\\,\\,{x}_{2}={360}^{∘}+x={360}^{∘}-{56.310}^{∘}={303.690}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n123.690\\cdot 303.690≈37\\,563\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-120626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "287347f118da8b2a84761a8f163ea985659aa04f8751834606c661b7961e7d35"} {"question" "q-120626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-120626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "33\\,126"] ["math" "35\\,986"] ["math" "37\\,563"] ["math" "43\\,245"] ["math" "44\\,278"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-120626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "37\\,563"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$  for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)=(63.434\\,9…)^{∘}≈{63.435}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${63.435}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is ${x}_{1}={63}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}+{63.435}^{∘}≈{243}^{∘},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={63}^{∘}$ and $x={243}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "287347f118da8b2a84761a8f163ea985659aa04f8751834606c661b7961e7d35"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "63"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-274561" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "243"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies in the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot \\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "287347f118da8b2a84761a8f163ea985659aa04f8751834606c661b7961e7d35"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006298/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1744550662681768072dfa23dc0592dcc82c1801464dd8aa279037253571c15"} {"question" "q-330609" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/math